Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  FinalType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  07 REMOVED_MONTH 00 ACCESSIONED ON  07 REMOVED_MONTH 00 at 18 58 CLINICAL DATA  Right breast carcinoma  positive margins for DCIS on initial exision                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  RIGHT   RE EXCISION  HEALING BIOPSY SITE  NO RESIDUAL CARCINOMA IS IDENTIFIED  BREAST  FINAL SHAVE MARGIN   EXCISION  HEALING BIOPSY SITE  THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY  Diagnosis by  SELIM NASSER  MD Signed On  10 REMOVED_MONTH 00 at 16 59 Staff Pathologist  John H  Eichhorn  M D                                                                                  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name and unit number only  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 3 0cm portion of fibrofatty breast  The specimen is oriented by the surgeon with a short suture designating superior and a long suture designating lateral  The specimen is inked as follows  posterior black  anterior blue  superior red  inferior yellow  lateral green  and medial orange  The specimen is sectioned to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm biopsy cavity which is lined by fat necrosis and fibrous tissue  The biopsy cavity comes to within 0 2cm from the superior margin and  0 1cm from the inferior posterior and anterior margin  No residual tumor is identified  Representative sections are submitted as follows  A  Biopsy cavity through posterior margin B  Biopsy cavity through anterior margin C  Biopsy cavity through inferior margin D  Biopsy cavity through superior margin E  Biopsy cavity through lateral margin F  Biopsy cavity through medial margin G  Additional posterior margin Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name and unit number  and  final shave margin   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm aggregate of fibrofatty breast tissue fragments which are entirely submitted in cassettes H L  Gross Description by  SHANNON SCHUTZ Resident  MELINDA J  FAN  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  SAME DAY SURGICAL UNIT    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REPORTS TO  Barbara Lynn Smith  M D  PART A  BREAST  RIGHT   RE EXCISION PART B  BREAST  FINAL SHAVE MARGIN  EXCISION , Unchecked.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  MS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE  MS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Breast CTR Surg WAC 240 MEEI MRN  REMOVED_DATE Path Subspecialty Service  Breast 1 Results To  CONNIE ROCHE  NP KENNY C LAI  M D  RACHEL ANN HITT  M D  Resident  Matija Snuderl  M D  Signed Out by  Ronald Balassanian  M D  CLINICAL DATA  The patient is a 56 year old woman with very dense breast with indeterminate focus of enhancement seen in the medial left breast on MRI  FINAL DIAGNOSIS  A  BREAST  LEFT   CORE BIOPSY 1  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  WELL DIFFERENTIATED GRADE REMOVED_DATE 2  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  DCIS   MICROPAPILLARY TYPE  GRADE REMOVED_DATE 3  FIBROCYSTIC CHANGE WITH APOCRINE METAPLASIA  SEE NOTE  Note  Sections of the core biopsy reveal a rare cluster of angulated ducts infiltrating the stroma which are present over an area measuring approximately 0 2cm  Adjacent to the lesion is a 0 3cm cluster of ducts which are expanded by a proliferation of monomorphic ductal cells arranged in a micropapillary pattern lining the duct structures  The morphologic findings are compatible with a diagnosis of invasive tubular carcinoma arising in association with low grade micropapillary ductal carcinoma in situ  To help confirm these diagnoses  we performed level sections and a panel of immunostains on the core biopsy  Multiple level sections show the DCIS to better advantage  An immunostain for keratin REMOVED_DATE shows focal loss of staining in luminal cells and supports the diagnosis of DCIS  Immunohistochemical stains for cytokeratin  p63  myosin and calponin performed on slide  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  demonstrate absence of the myoepithelial layer in the infiltrating ducts and supports the diagnosis of invasive ductal carcinoma  Overall  the findings are limited due to limited sampling of the lesion  however  based on morphology and immunohistochemistry  the findings are compatible with well differentiated invasive ductal carcinoma and low grade ductal carcinoma in situ  Dr  David Wilbur has reviewed this case and concurs with the diagnosis  GROSS DESCRIPTION  The specimen is received in formalin   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME   REMOVE_MRN only is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 1cm  aggregate of multiple tan yellow to gray white fibroadipose tissue cores  which are submitted in toto  in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Final Diagnosis by Ronald Balassanian M D   Electronically signed on Monday August 31   REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 12 51 13PM By his her signature above  the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses  PART   Breast  Left    Core biopsy  report_end  , Unchecked.
